newRoadMarkT -- full monty step 2: the `newRoad` mark (Tony, 2026-11-20).

A bare `newRoad` on an action's definition makes processCode read its body on kantGrammar's STARt instead of
the old grammar's BlocK. A marked body that does not build there -- a statement refused in the build counts --
is refused naming the action, and is NOT read on the old grammar. Unmarked bodies are untouched.

The road proof is `7 #5d`: kantGrammar prints `[ 7 ]`, the old grammar `[     7 ]`. nmGood and nmGoodOld are
one body, marked and unmarked; nmGood is called twice (a cached body reached a second time). nmBad and
nmBadOld are one body with the old for-filter spelling, which the old grammar builds and kantGrammar refuses:
the unmarked twin walks a b c, the marked one is refused by name, prints nothing, and is refused again on its
second call (it never gets a BlocK). The run goes on past each refusal to the sentinel.
